message.reasoning_content:
We need to write 17 Reslil programs. Let me carefully design each one, respecting Reslil quirks:

- All binary operators same precedence, right-to-left grouping. So always parenthesize.
- Division rounds toward negative infinity (floor). Remainder follows.
- `dilisyo`/`emsoulve` evaluate both operands, require booleans.
- No truthiness.
- Lists shared/mutable.
- `cha` in top-level ends program.
- String indexing gives one-char string.
- `odresdra` converts string to integer.
- `diwela()` reads next line.
- `ramou(v)` converts to print string.
- `louwo` length.
- `omse(xs, v)` appends.
- Switch statement `moumare amdre expr amdre literals ... statements ... amdre oli ... bawoum moumare`.
- Functions declared at top level, can be called before declaration.
- Statements end with `;`.
- Comments start with `'`.

Important: comparisons chain issue — `a < b` returns boolean; can't do `a < b < c`.

Let me write each task.

### t0-count
Read n, print 1..n one per line.

```
sheye line <- diwela();
wesosa (line <> malwe) {
    sheye n <- odresdra(line);
    sho (sheye i <- 1; i <= n; i <- i + 1) {
        wami i;
    }
}
```

Input always has one line. Fine.

### t0-day-names
Read lines until EOF, print day name.

```
tosedo dayname(d)
    moumare amdre d
    amdre 1
        cha "Monday";
    amdre 2
        cha "Tuesday";
    amdre 3
        cha "Wednesday";
    amdre 4
        cha "Thursday";
    amdre 5
        cha "Friday";
    amdre 6
        cha "Saturday";
    amdre 7
        cha "Sunday";
    amdre oli
        cha "invalid";
    bawoum moumare
bawoum tosedo

sheye line <- diwela();
veshim (line <> malwe) {
    wami dayname(odresdra(line));
    line <- diwela();
}
```

Wait — inside `moumare`, `cha` inside amdre blocks. Can `cha` be used there? `cha` ends the function. The switch is inside a function so `cha` should exit the function. The reference says `cha` ends the function and gives back the value. Since switch doesn't create functions, cha inside switch inside function should end the function. That seems plausible. But maybe safer to compute a string var then return:

```
tosedo dayname(d)
    sheye r <- "invalid";
    moumare amdre d
    amdre 1
        r <- "Monday";
    ...
    bawoum moumare
    cha r;
bawoum tosedo
```

Both should work. Actually `cha` directly is fine — the examples show `wesosa` with `cha` inside in fact:

```
tosedo fact(n)
    wesosa (n <= 1) {
        cha 1;
    }
    cha n * fact(n - 1);
```

Yes, `cha` inside `wesosa` works. So `cha` inside `moumare` should too.

### t0-even-odd
```
sheye line <- diwela();
sheye even <- 0;
sheye odd <- 0;
veshim (line <> malwe) {
    sheye n <- odresdra(line);
    wesosa (n drossou 2 = 0) {
        even <- even + 1;
    } oli {
        odd <- odd + 1;
    }
    line <- diwela();
}
wami "even " + even;
wami "odd " + odd;
```

Careful: `n drossou 2 = 0` — precedence all same, right-to-left: `n drossou (2 = 0)` = `n drossou false` → error. Need parentheses: `(n drossou 2) = 0`. Important!

Also `wami "even " + even` → grouping: `("even " + even)` since + is right assoc but single op fine. Good.

