### t0-reverse-lines
Read all lines into a list, then print reversed.

```
sheye lines <- [];
sheye line <- diwela();
veshim (line <> malwe) {
    omse(lines, line);
    line <- diwela();
}
sheye i <- louwo(lines) - 1;
veshim (i >= 0) {
    wami lines[i];
    i <- i - 1;
}
```

Careful: `louwo(lines) - 1` fine.

### t1-digits
Sum of decimal digits of n, and reversed digits as integer keeping sign. n up to 1e15 in absolute value, fits in 64-bit. But careful: odresdra of "-4500" gives -4500. Reversed integer: e.g., -4500 → digits 4,5,0,0 reversed "0045" → 45 with sign → -45. Wait expected output: `-4500` gives `9 -54`. Hmm wait: sum of digits of 4500 = 9, reversed digits "0054" → 54, sign → -54. Yes.

Approach: read line string. Determine sign. Work with absolute value as integer? abs up to 10^15 fits. Then repeatedly take mod 10 and divide by 10 (floor division fine for non-negative). Build reversed integer and sum digits. Special-case 0.

But careful with "-0"? Input integers presumably standard; abs value at most 1e15. Could input be "-0"? Probably not. Let's handle sign based on first char.

```
sheye line <- diwela();
veshim (line <> malwe) {
    sheye neg <- ishe;
    sheye s <- line;
    wesosa (louwo(s) > 0) {
        wesosa (s[0] = "-") {
            neg <- shilsa;
            s <- s - ... 
```

Hmm, no substring. Better to use integer arithmetic: `n <- odresdra(line)`, then if n < 0, work with -n, remembering sign.

```
sheye line <- diwela();
veshim (line <> malwe) {
    sheye n <- odresdra(line);
    sheye neg <- ishe;
    wesosa (n < 0) {
        neg <- shilsa;
        n <- -n;
    }
    sheye sum <- 0;
    sheye rev <- 0;
    wesosa (n = 0) {
        sum <- 0;
        rev <- 0;
    }
    vesheim (n > 0) {
        sheye d <- n drossou 10;
        sum <- sum + d;
        rev <- rev * 10 + d;
        n <- n / 10;
    }
    wesosa (neg) {
        rev <- -rev;
    }
    wami sum + " " + rev;
    line <- diwela();
}
```

Careful: `wami sum + " " + rev` → `(sum + " ") + rev` = string concat. Good. `rev <- rev * 10 + d` — precedence! `*` and `+` same precedence right-to-left: `rev * (10 + d)`. WRONG. Need `rev <- (rev * 10) + d;`. Very important.

Also `sheye d <- n drossou 10;` fine. `n <- n / 10;` fine.

Also if n = 0 and neg... "-0" would give rev = -0 = 0, sum 0. Output "0 0". Fine.

### t1-floordiv
Reslil `/` already rounds toward negative infinity, and drossou gives sign of b (divisor). Let's verify: `7 / 2 = 3`, `7 drossou 2 = 1`. `-7 / 2 = -4`, `-7 drossou 2 = 1` (a - b*q = -7 -2*(-4) = 1). Yes matches "r is 0 or has sign of b". `7 -2`: 7/(-2) = -4 (floor), r = 7 - (-2)(-4) = 7-8 = -1. Matches. So just:

```
sheye line <- diwela();
veshim (line <> malwe) {
