    cha ishe;
bawoum tosedo
```

Note in lowerc, `cha` inside for loop — cha ends function. Fine.

Note `(c = lo[i]) emsoulve (c = up[i])` parenthesized. Good.

Careful with `cha isletter(c);` — fine.

Empty filtered string: loop doesn't run, ok stays shilsa → "yes". Good.

### t1-rank
First line: sorted list of ints. Then queries. For each query: count of ints < query, and found/missing.

Since sorted, count smaller via binary search or linear. At most 1000, linear fine. But parsing first line: numbers separated by single spaces. Need split-by-space. Write a tokenizer producing list of ints from a line:

```
tosedo splitints(s)
    sheye r <- [];
    sheye cur <- "";
    sheye hascur <- ishe;
    sho (sheye i <- 0; i <= louwo(s); i <- i + 1) {
        wesosa (i = louwo(s)) {
            wesosa (hascur) {
                omse(r, odresdra(cur));
            }
        } oli wesosa (s[i] = " ") {
            wesosa (hascur) {
                omse(r, odresdra(cur));
                cur <- "";
                hascur <- ishe;
            }
        } oli {
            cur <- cur + s[i];
            hascur <- shilsa;
        }
    }
    cha r;
bawoum tosedo
```

Handles multiple spaces too. First line could be empty (zero integers) — then diwela returns "" and splitints returns []. Good.

Wait — if first line is empty string, `i = louwo(s)` = 0 = louwo, hascur false, returns []. Good.

For each query line: n smaller = count of xs[i] < q; found = any xs[i] = q. Linear:

```
sheye first <- diwela();
sheye xs <- splitints(first);
sheye line <- diwela();
veshim (line <> malwe) {
    sheye q <- odresdra(line);
    sheye smaller <- 0;
    sheye found <- ishe;
    sho (sheye i <- 0; i < louwo(xs); i <- i + 1) {
        wesosa (xs[i] < q) {
            smaller <- smaller + 1;
        }
        wesosa (xs[i] = q) {
            found <- shilsa;
        }
    }
    wami smaller + " " + (found ? "found" : "missing");
    line <- diwela();
}
```

No ternary. Use conditional:

```
    wesosa (found) {
        wami smaller + " found";
    } oli {
        wami smaller + " missing";
    }
```

### t1-rotate
First line k (|k| ≤ 1e9), then items. Rotate right by k. With m items, effective shift k mod m (if m > 0). Negative k rotates left. Compute shift = ((k mod m) + m) mod m — need careful because drossou sign follows... In Reslil, `a drossou b` = a - b*(a/b) floor-based, so sign follows b (divisor). `k drossou m` with m>0 gives result in [0, m-1]? Let's check: floor division, r = a - b*floor(a/b), with b>0, 0 ≤ r < b. Yes for b>0 remainder is non-negative. So shift = k drossou m works for negative k too: -1 drossou 3 = -1 - 3*(-1) = 2. Yes, since -1/3 = -1 (floor), r = -1+3=2. Good.

So shift = k drossou m, in [0, m-1]. Rotated output: items[m-shift .. m-1] then items[0 .. m-shift-1]. If shift = 0, all in order. 

Edge: k=0 → shift 0. m=0 → print nothing.

```
sheye kline <- diwela();
sheye k <- 0;
wesosa (kline <> malwe) {
    k <- odresdra(kline);
}
sheye items <- [];
sheye line <- diwela();
veshim (line <> malwe) {
    omse(items, line);
    line <- diwela();
}
sheye m <- louwo(items);
wesosa (m > 0) {
    sheye shift <- k drossou m;
    sho (sheye i <- 0; i < m; i <- i + 1) {
        wami items[(i + m - shift) drossou m];
    }
}
```

Check: i from 0..m-1, index (i + m - shift) mod m. For i=0: m-shift mod m = (m-shift) if shift>0 else 0. Good. That prints last `shift` items first. Check shift=1, m=3: indices: (0+3-1)%3=2, (1+2)%3=0, (2+2)%3=1 → items[2], items[0], items[1]. Rotating right by 1 moves last to front. Correct.

`(i + m - shift) drossou m` precedence: all left-to-right? No—right to left: `i + (m - shift)` wait: operators +, -, drossou all same precedence, right assoc: `i + m - shift drossou m` would parse as `i + (m - (shift drossou m))`. Wrong! Parenthesize: `((i + m - shift) drossou m)` — inside, `i + m - shift` parses right-to-left: `i + (m - shift)`. Since addition/subtraction... `i + (m - shift)` = i+m-shift. Correct result. OK but be explicit: `(((i + m) - shift) drossou m)`. Fine.

Input spec: first line always holds k. "It is followed by zero or more lines". So kline always present. Simplify:

```
sheye k <- odresdra(diwela());
```

But what if input has zero lines total? "The first line holds k", so at least one line. I'll still guard. Simpler to assume it exists. I'll do `sheye k <- odresdra(diwela());`.

### t1-weekday
Gregorian calendar, dates 0001-01-01 to 9999-12-31. Compute day of week. Known: 0001-01-01 was a Monday. Let's verify: The reference example says 2000-01-01 is Saturday. Days since 0001-01-01 mod 7: if 0001-01-01 = Monday (day 0), then day of week = days mod 7, 0=Monday,...,6=Sunday.

Check 2000-01-01: days from 0001-01-01 to 2000-01-01 = (2000-1)*365 + number of leap days in between. Leap years from 1 to 1999: floor(1999/4) - floor(1999/100) + floor(1999/400) = 499 - 19 + 4 = 484. Total days = 1999*365 + 484 = 729635 + 484 = 730119. 730119 mod 7 = ? 730119 / 7 = 104302.71..., 7*104302 = 730114, remainder 5. Day 5 = Saturday (0=Mon,1=Tue,2=Wed,3=Thu,4=Fri,5=Sat). Yes! Matches.

Algorithm: parse year y, month m, day d. Compute days = (y-1)*365 + leapdays_before_y + day_of_year - 1, where leapdays_before_y = floor((y-1)/4) - floor((y-1)/100) + floor((y-1)/400). Then weekday = days mod 7 (non-negative since days ≥ 0).

Day of year: sum days in months before m, plus d, accounting leap for February.

Implement with helper `is leap`:

```
tosedo isleap(y)
    cha (((y drossou 4) = 0) dilisyo (((y drossou 100) = 0) emsoulve ((y drossou 400) = 0)));
```

Careful: `dilisyo`/`emsoulve` need boolean operands — comparisons are booleans, fine. Precedence: everything right-to-left same. Let me write fully parenthesized:

leap = ((y drossou 4) = 0) dilisyo ((((y drossou 100) = 0) emsoulve ((y drossou 400) = 0)))

Hmm the standard formula: leap if (div by 4) AND (not div by 100 OR div by 400). So:

`(y mod 4 = 0) and ((y mod 100 <> 0) or (y mod 400 = 0))`.

Write:

```
tosedo isleap(y)
    sheye a <- (y drossou 4) = 0;
    sheye b <- (y drossou 100) <> 0;
    sheye c <- (y drossou 400) = 0;
    cha (a dilisyo (b emsoulve c));
bawoum tosedo
```

Parse date: year = odresdra(slice(line,0,4)), month = odresdra(slice(line,5,7)), day = odresdra(slice(line,8,10)).

dayofyear: days in months. Use cumulative approach:

```
sheye doy <- d;
sho (sheye mm <- 1; mm < m; mm <- mm + 1) {
    doy <- doy + mdays(y, mm);
}
```

mdays: 31,28/29,31,30,31,30,31,31,30,31,30,31. Use moumare on mm:

```
tosedo mdays(y, m)
    moumare amdre m
    amdre 2
        wesosa (isleap(y)) {
            cha 29;
        }
        cha 28;
    amdre 4, 6, 9, 11
        cha 30;
    amdre oli
        cha 31;
    bawoum moumare
bawoum tosedo
```

Wait — `cha` inside `wesosa` inside `moumare` inside function. Cha should propagate out. Should be fine.

Days before year y:

```
sheye ymdays <- (y - 1) * 365;
sheye leaps <- ((y - 1) / 4) - ((y - 1) / 100) + ((y - 1) / 400);
```

Precedence!! `((y - 1) / 4) - ((y - 1) / 100) + ((y - 1) / 400)` — parse right-to-left: A - B + C = A - (B + C)? Right assoc: `A - (B + C)`. That gives A - B - C — WRONG. Must parenthesize: `(((y - 1) / 4) - ((y - 1) / 100)) + ((y - 1) / 400)`.

Since y ≥ 1, y-1 ≥ 0, floor division fine.

weekday index = (ymdays + leaps + doy - 1) drossou 7 → 0..6. Then map to name via index into list or moumare.

Names: Monday..Sunday.

```
sheye names <- ["Monday","Tuesday","Wednesday","Thursday","Friday","Saturday","Sunday"];
wami names[wd];
```

Nice, no switch needed.

Full:

```
tosedo isleap(y)
    sheye a <- (y drossou 4) = 0;
    sheye b <- (y drossou 100) <> 0;
    sheye c <- (y drossou 400) = 0;
    cha (a dilisyo (b emsoulve c));
bawoum tosedo

tosedo mdays(y, m)
    moumare amdre m
    amdre 2
        wesosa (isleap(y)) {
            cha 29;
        }
        cha 28;
    amdre 4, 6, 9, 11
        cha 30;
    amdre oli
        cha 31;
    bawoum moumare
bawoum tosedo

tosedo slice(s, i, j)
    sheye r <- "";
    sho (sheye k <- i; k < j; k <- k + 1) {
        r <- r + s[k];
    }
    cha r;
bawoum tosedo

sheye names <- ["Monday", "Tuesday", "Wednesday", "Thursday", "Friday", "Saturday", "Sunday"];
sheye line <- diwela();
veshim (line <> malwe) {
    sheye y <- odresdra(slice(line, 0, 4));
    sheye m <- odresdra(slice(line, 5, 7));
    sheye d <- odresdra(slice(line, 8, 10));
    sheye doy <- d;
    sho (sheye mm <- 1; mm < m; mm <- mm + 1) {
        doy <- doy + mdays(y, mm);
    }
    sheye days <- ((y - 1) * 365) + (((((y - 1) / 4) - ((y - 1) / 100)) + ((y - 1) / 400)) + (doy - 1));
    sheye wd <- days drossou 7;
    wami names[wd];
    line <- diwela();
}
```

Check `((y - 1) * 365)` fine. days ≥ 0 always (for 0001-01-01: ymdays=0, leaps=0, doy=1, days=0). wd = 0 → Monday. Good.

### t2-bignum
Big integers up to 100 digits. Implement decimal string addition and multiplication.

Addition: standard digit-by-digit from right. Represent strings. Helper to add:

```
tosedo addstr(a, b)
    sheye i <- louwo(a) - 1;
    sheye j <- louwo(b) - 1;
    sheye carry <- 0;
    sheye r <- "";
    vesheim ((i >= 0) emsoulve (j >= 0)) {
        sheye da <- 0;
        wesosa (i >= 0) {
            da <- odresdra(a[i]);
        }
        sheye db <- 0;
        wesosa (j >= 0) {
            db <- odresdra(b[j]);
        }
        sheye s <- da + db + carry;
        wesosa (s >= 10) {
            s <- s - 10;
            carry <- 1;
        } oli {
            carry <- 0;
        }
        r <- ramou(s) + r;
        i <- i - 1;
        j <- j - 1;
    }
    wesosa (carry = 1) {
        r <- "1" + r;
    }
    cha r;
bawoum tosedo
```

Note `ramou(s)` for single digit s. But could build digit string from a digits string: `"0123456789"[s]` cleaner and faster. Use that: `r <- "0123456789"[s] + r;`

